Date: Fri, 9 Jul 93 10:21:48 -0700
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: NANO: flagella dimensions and operations

I don't have all the dimensions that Mike Price wanted from Perry, but
the recent college upper-division microbiology text, _Microbiology_,
_Fundamentals_and_Applications_, 2nd. ed., by Ronald M. Atlas,
(Macmillan, 1988) has a discussion of prokaryotic and eukaryotic
flagella and cilia on pages 72-79, with photographs at stated
magnifications, showing these structures.  Measuring and scaling shows
bacterial flagella a few tens of nanometers wide.  Other photos also
show bacterial attachment and genetic-interchange structures called
_pili_ (singular _pilus_) which are somewhat narrower.  The text
accompanying figure 3.34 describes the biological mechanism whereby
a bacterial flagellum is made to rotate, in quite mechanistic terms,
with bearings, rods and whatnot.  The bearings are stated to be
0.2 micron diameter, the flagellum to be 17 nanometers diameter
(checking my scaling).  The energy for rotation is said to be
derived from hydrogen-ion transport across the cell's
cytoplasmic membrane (other parts of the cell serve to establish
an H-ion density gradient across the membrane, which various
cellular mechanisms can then use as an energy source).  The
energy budget for flagella rotation is given as about 256 H-ion
transports per revolution.

I suspect that most microbiology texts would contain similar
information, try your local college bookstore.

                                                -- Jay Freeman

