Date: Fri, 9 Jul 93 16:06:08 -0400
From: pavel@park.bu.edu (Paul Cisek)
Subject: NANO: Coordinating General Assemblers

Derek Zahn writes:
>
>If we can have equipment around the vat, it might be possible to
>communicate position and command electromagnetically from outside
>with phased-array antennas, but I don't know if the resolution
>would be good enough (pity if not, because a "matter transmitter"
>could work this way).
>
>Has anybody seen proposals for the way assemblers coordinate?

I suspect that instead of coordinating the process from the outside, we can
let the assemblers self-organize their behavior.  For example, we can let
homogenous networks of assemblers differentiate their function through
competition (much like caste selection among social insects - the first queen
to be born produces pheromones that prevent other embryos from developing into
a queen...).  The assemblers can use various chemical or force gradients (such
as gravity) to "decide" if they take one function or another.  They can also
simply use an asymmetric distribution of building materials.  Construction of
tubes can be done by folding sheets of assemblers simply by having the end
members attract each other...

The downside to such a production process is that it requires the designs to
tolerate developmental noise.  Also, we will be rather constrained in the sorts
of things we can build - precise clockwork will be difficult using chemical
gradients and such.  The upside is that we have a great deal of good examples
to follow by looking at some of nature's solutions, and learn to come up with
even more clever ones.  Also, we can perhaps train our assembler systems using
evolutionary methods, where the fitness function depends on the quality of the
finished product.

Paul


