> Generalizing about human nature from primate studies is an excersize in
> futility.

Actually, I think it can be done right, and usefully.  For example:

> How? Pick your primate! Baboons? Oh, we're all heirarchal pecking order
> displaying idiots-- can't help it; get with it and do it well...

You don't have to go all the way to monkeys (which baboons are).  This
is the standard story about chimps (by which I mean chimps, not bonobos).
But I've seen a fairly well-documented argument (I don't have the reference
handy, but I'll track it down if anybody wants it) that they show this
behavior only under certain conditions.
     It turns out that chimp groups also vary in the extent of hierarchy
formation.  Apparently when chimps live in conditions in which individuals
can pack up and leave (OK, chimps don't "pack up"), dominance relations are 
pretty loose, but when a group for some reason is confined to a specific
area the hierarchy crystalizes and we get the classic dominance hierarchy.
When does this happen?  Well, for example, if the group is being studied by
primatologists who keep them handy for study by regularly supplying food at
a specific location.  (So why is the standard story about chimp behavior
so hierarchy-oriented?  Aha!)
     Now, looking for human parallels, this is exactly the
difference between non-hierarchical human societies--a pattern found
pretty much only (but pretty much always) in nomadic hunter-gatherers--and
others, all others being agricultural, and thus bound to specific locations.
Could be there's an interesting generalization here ...

> My favorite primate? Bonobos monkeys.

Apes.  Yeah, they're everybody's favorite, these days, as they seem
to have so much in common with humans. 

> They have sex constantly. Its the primary method of social bonding.

Which is *really* interesting, given that the origin of continuous
sexuality is one of the major puzzles in human evolution (and, 
arguably, one of the major determinants of human social structure).

